{
  "id": "v06",
  "title": "Spelling Rules & Confusing Misspellings",
  "intro": "English orthography is notoriously irregular, yet recurring exam spellings conform to identifiable phonetic patterns, prefix/suffix guidelines, and consonant doubling rules. In the WBPSC Clerkship examination, spelling questions specifically target words with double consonants (c, m, s, r, l), silent letters, vowels before suffixes (-able vs -ible), and the classic 'i before e' rule.",
  "bengali": "Spelling (বানান শুদ্ধিকরণ): WBPSC Clerkship পরীক্ষায় বানান সংক্রান্ত প্রশ্নে যেসব শব্দ আসে, সেগুলোতে সাধারণত Double Consonant (যেমন: Accommodation-এ double c এবং double m, Embarrass-এ double r এবং double s) অথবা 'ei' বনাম 'ie'-এর বিভ্রান্তি তৈরি করা হয়।\n\nWBPSC Clerkship-এ ভালো নম্বর পাওয়ার জন্য ৪টি মৌলিক বানানের নিয়ম:\n১. 'I before E, except after C':\n   - সাধারণ ক্ষেত্রে 'ie' বসে: Believe, achieve, relief, thief, chief, brief, yield।\n   - কিন্তু 'C'-এর ঠিক পরে হলে 'ei' বসে: Receive, deceive, conceive, ceiling, receipt।\n   - ব্যতিক্রম (Exceptions): Weird, seize, leisure, protein, height, foreign।\n২. Consonant দ্বিত্ব হওয়ার নিয়ম (Doubling Rule):\n   শব্দে এক বা দুটি সিলেবল থাকলে এবং শেষ অক্ষর Consonant এবং তার ঠিক আগের অক্ষরটি একক Vowel হলে, Vowel দিয়ে শুরু হওয়া Suffix (-ed, -ing, -er, -ence) যোগ করার সময় শেষের Consonant-টি দ্বিত্ব (Double) হয়:\n   - Occur -> Occurred, Occurrence\n   - Begin -> Beginning\n   - Refer -> Referred, Referral\n   - Commit -> Committed, Committee (Committee-তে double m, double t, double e!)\n৩. শেষের 'E' বাদ পড়ার নিয়ম:\n   শব্দের শেষে অনুচ্চারিত 'e' থাকলে, Vowel দিয়ে শুরু হওয়া প্রত্যয় যোগের সময় 'e' বাদ যায়:\n   - Hope + ing = Hoping; Guide + ance = Guidance; Argue + ment = Argument (লক্ষ্য করুন: Argument-এ 'e' নেই!)।\n   - কিন্তু Consonant দিয়ে প্রত্যয় শুরু হলে 'e' থেকে যায়: Hope + ful = Hopeful; Nine + ty = Ninety (ব্যতিক্রম: Ninth)।\n৪. -able বনাম -ible:\n   - মূল শব্দটি যদি নিজে একটি সম্পূর্ণ শব্দ হয়, তবে সাধারণত '-able' যুক্ত হয়: Accept + able = Acceptable, Read + able = Readable।\n   - মূল শব্দটি যদি একা কোনো অর্থপূর্ণ শব্দ না হয়, তবে '-ible' যুক্ত হয়: Audible, visible, edible, illegible, incredible।",
  "rules": [
    {
      "rule": "THE 'I BEFORE E EXCEPT AFTER C' RULE: Use 'IE' for the /i:/ sound in general words, but use 'EI' immediately following the letter 'C'.",
      "examples": [
        "IE after other consonants: Believe, achieve, relief, thief, brief, grief, yield.",
        "EI after the letter 'C': Receive, deceive, conceive, ceiling, receipt, perceive."
      ],
      "exceptions": [
        "Common exam exceptions: Weird, seize, leisure, caffeine, protein, height, foreign, neighbor."
      ]
    },
    {
      "rule": "CONSONANT DOUBLING WITH STRESSED VOWELS: A final single consonant preceded by a single vowel doubles when receiving a vowel-initial suffix if the final syllable is stressed.",
      "examples": [
        "Occur -> Occurrence, Occurred (Double 'c' and double 'r').",
        "Commit -> Committee (Double 'm', double 't', double 'e'), Committed.",
        "Equip -> Equipped, Equipping; Begin -> Beginning; Run -> Running."
      ],
      "exceptions": [
        "If the accent/stress is on the first syllable, the consonant does NOT double in British English: Offer -> Offered, Offering; Prefer -> Preference (stress shifts back to 'pref-')."
      ]
    },
    {
      "rule": "DROPPING THE SILENT FINAL 'E': Silent final 'e' is dropped before vowel suffixes (-ing, -able, -ation), but retained before consonant suffixes (-ful, -ment, -ly).",
      "examples": [
        "Drop 'e' before vowel: Guide + ance = Guidance; Argue + ment = Argument; True + ly = Truly.",
        "Retain 'e' before consonant: Hope + ful = Hopeful; Manage + ment = Management; Sincere + ly = Sincerely."
      ],
      "exceptions": [
        "Soft 'c' and 'g' retain 'e' before 'a' and 'o' to maintain their soft pronunciation: Courageous, Noticeable, Peaceable."
      ]
    },
    {
      "rule": "SUFFIX PAIRINGS: '-ABLE' vs '-IBLE': If the base is an intact independent English word, it typically takes '-able'; if an incomplete root, it takes '-ible'.",
      "examples": [
        "Intact base takes -ABLE: Dependable, readable, acceptable, comfortable, perishable.",
        "Incomplete root takes -IBLE: Visible, audible, edible, credible, eligible, illegible."
      ],
      "exceptions": [
        "Inevitable, formidable, culpable are established exceptions using -able on Latin bases."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "Writing 'Accomodation' instead of 'Accommodation' (must have double 'c' AND double 'm').",
    "Writing 'Embarass' instead of 'Embarrass' (must have double 'r' AND double 's').",
    "Writing 'Occurence' instead of 'Occurrence' (must have double 'r').",
    "Writing 'Seperate' instead of 'Separate' (remember: there is 'A RAT' in separate).",
    "Writing 'Definately' instead of 'Definitely' (from finite -> definitely, with 'i')."
  ],
  "examTricks": [
    "The 'A RAT' Trick for SEPARATE: Sep-A-RAT-e (never write 'seper-').",
    "The 'Committee' Rule: 2 M's, 2 T's, 2 E's -> Co-mm-i-tt-ee.",
    "The 'Truly / Argument' Check: True drops 'e' -> TRULY. Argue drops 'e' -> ARGUMENT.",
    "The 'Accommodation' Rule: 2 Cots, 2 Mattresses -> 2 C's, 2 M's."
  ],
  "memoryTricks": [
    "There is A RAT in sep-A-RAT-e.",
    "Accommodation has 2 Cars and 2 Motors (CC + MM)."
  ],
  "revision": [
